# Batch43
note('alg-na-romberg-trapezoid-table','Romberg defines a triangular table and top value, with positive Richardson denominators; it supplies no equivalence test.',empty='C:At L=0 the only table member is R_0,0=T_1; both Richardson loops have no iterations.',zero='C:The zero-level table has N_0=1 and returns its single composite-trapezoid entry.',one='C:With L=1 the entries are T_1,T_2 and R_1,1=T_2+(T_2-T_1)/3.',degenerate='N:The input interval is explicitly nondegenerate, so b=a is outside the Romberg table domain.',endpoints='C:Each base column is the closed composite trapezoid and therefore includes the supplied values f(a),f(b).',choice='N:Both table-index sets are explicitly bounded by supplied L; no selection of unspecified quadrature data is made.')
note('def-na-bernoulli-polynomials','Bernoulli polynomials are fixed by a coefficient recursion, not by a biconditional characterization.',empty='C:At index m=0 the separate assignment B_0=1 precedes every coefficient sum.',zero='C:The Bernoulli recursion initializes B_0(X)=1; it does not apply the predecessor recursion at zero.',one='C:Putting m=1 and beta_0,0=1 in the recursion gives B_1(X)=X-1/2.',degenerate='N:This is a formal-polynomial recursion; it has no mesh cell, variable interval, or geometric collapse parameter.',endpoints='N:Evaluation B_m(0) defines a number, rather than an endpoint condition on an integration domain.',choice='N:Every coefficient of B_m is determined from the finite coefficient list of B_(m-1).')
note('def-na-clenshaw-curtis-dct-i','DCT-I is an explicit linear transform with an unnormalised Lobatto convention, not an equivalence statement.',empty='C:For n=1 the interior sum j=1,...,n-1 is empty; the two endpoint summands remain.',zero='N:DCT-I explicitly excludes n=0 and justifies division by the positive natural n.',one='C:For n=1 the formula gives X_0=y_0+y_1 and X_1=y_0-y_1.',degenerate='N:The fixed Lobatto reference coordinates and n>=1 leave distinct endpoint nodes; no zero-width interval is an input.',endpoints='C:The ordered nodes run from cos(0)=1 to cos(pi)=-1 and their data have weights 1 and (-1)^k.',choice='N:The transform consumes a specified vector of n+1 samples and computes n+1 finite sums.')
note('def-na-closed-newton-cotes-eight-panel-rule','The nine-node Newton-Cotes rule fixes rational weights and a finite evaluation sum; it asserts no converse.',empty='N:The rule has exactly nine nodes j=0,...,8, so no empty node set is permitted.',zero='C:The zero function has all nine samples zero, hence the defined weighted sum is zero.',one='C:For the constant function one, the nine displayed rational weights sum to two; the rule remains a nine-node rule.',degenerate='N:Its domain is the fixed interval [-1,1], with eight panels of width 1/4.',endpoints='C:The j=0 and j=8 nodes are -1 and1, with endpoint weight989/14175 at each.',choice='N:The node and weight arrays are written explicitly; the definition selects no unspecified nodes.')
note('def-na-peano-kernel','The Peano construction defines L and K_r for a finite quadrature and r>=1; polynomial exactness is a predicate, not an iff theorem.',empty='C:If the finite node list is empty, Q is the empty sum zero and L is the interval integral; the kernel definition remains meaningful.',zero='N:The derivative order r is assumed at least1; no factorial of a negative predecessor is evaluated.',one='C:The r=1 branch is the indicator of (t,b], with denominator0!=1; its Darboux integrability is established separately.',degenerate='N:The Peano kernel domain explicitly requires a<b.',endpoints='C:At t=b the truncated-power function is zero; for r=1 the definition separately treats the jump and finite node-value changes.',choice='N:Nodes and weights are supplied as a finite list, and the indicator partition uses a single epsilon-dependent witness.')
note('lem-na-chebyshev-degree-bound-from-recurrence','The Chebyshev support result bounds degree above by n; it does not characterize polynomials having that bound.',empty='N:The quantified Chebyshev polynomial T_n exists for every natural n; there is no input coefficient family that can be absent.',zero='C:Proof1.1 initializes T_0=1, whose support has no index above0.',one='C:Proof1.1 also initializes T_1=X, with no coefficient beyond index1.',degenerate='N:The support induction is algebraic and has no geometric cell or interval degeneracy.',endpoints='N:The claimed coefficient support is not restricted to an interval with boundary values.',choice='N:Proof1.2 forms T_(k+2) from the two fixed predecessor polynomials; induction makes no witness-family selection.')
note('lem-na-chebyshev-recurrence-angle-identity','The angle lemma evaluates T_n(cos theta) as cos(n theta); it does not state a necessary-and-sufficient predicate.',empty='N:A fixed natural index and real angle are supplied; the simultaneous induction uses two present polynomials.',zero='C:Proof1.1 gives T_0(cos theta)=1=cos0.',one='C:Proof1.1 gives T_1(cos theta)=cos theta before the recurrence step.',degenerate='N:Theta is any real; no nondegenerate cell or nonzero-angle hypothesis is imposed.',endpoints='C:The universal real theta includes0 and pi; recurrence and angle formulas remain defined at both.',choice='N:Proof1.2 substitutes two predecessor identities in a fixed polynomial/trigonometric recurrence.')
note('lem-na-bernoulli-polynomial-identities','The Bernoulli lemma gives derivative, mean, reflection and endpoint consequences of its recursion, rather than an iff criterion.',empty='N:For m>=1 the coefficient indices include q=0; B_0 is separately assigned for the reflection base.',zero='C:Proof1.2 starts reflection with B_0=1; derivative and zero-mean assertions are explicitly restricted to m>=1.',one='C:Proof1.1 computes B_1=x-1/2, whose derivative is1 and whose integral over[0,1] is0.',degenerate='N:The reference integration interval has length1; no variable interval is allowed to collapse.',endpoints='C:Proof2.1 gives B_m(1)=B_m(0) only for m>=2, avoiding the unequal endpoint values of B_1.',choice='N:Reflection uses induction and an explicitly defined difference polynomial D_m; its constant is determined by its zero integral.')
note('lem-na-chebyshev-integral-moments','The moment identity separates zero, odd and positive even frequency; its word equivalently just reindexes the even formula.',empty='N:The moment is an integral of a specified T_k on[-1,1], not an empty node-family sum.',zero='C:Proof2.1 computes mu_0 as the integral of sin theta on[0,pi], equal to2.',one='C:At k=1 the zero-frequency sine contribution is identically zero and the odd-frequency case gives mu_1=0.',degenerate='N:The change-of-variable intervals[-1,1] and[0,pi] have fixed positive lengths.',endpoints='C:Proof1.1 changes variables across the full closed cosine interval; Proof2.1 evaluates sine antiderivatives at0 and pi.',choice='N:The three frequency cases use explicit integrals and algebraic reindexing, with no choice of a polynomial or quadrature.')
note('lem-na-clenshaw-curtis-dct-i-orthogonality','DCT-I orthogonality lists diagonal and off-diagonal values of S_kl; it does not assert an iff predicate.',empty='C:At n=1 all interior j-sums vanish, leaving the two half-endpoint terms.',zero='N:The grid denominator n is positive by the explicit n>=1 assumption; zero frequency is treated as an endpoint frequency.',one='C:When n=1, both diagonal entries are endpoint cases with S_00=S_11=1, and S_01=0.',degenerate='N:The positive node count excludes collapse of the DCT grid; endpoint frequencies are handled rather than divided away.',endpoints='C:Proof3.1 distinguishes k=l=0 or n, where S=n, from interior diagonal entries S=n/2.',choice='N:Proof3.1 classifies the supplied pair of frequency indices using two explicitly defined finite cosine sums.')
note('thm-na-peano-kernel-remainder-and-bound','Peano representation and its norm bound follow from polynomial exactness and C^r regularity; no converse regularity claim is made.',zero='N:The order r>=1 and positive factorial denominators exclude a zero-order predecessor formula.',one='C:Proof3.2 handles r=1 separately by the piecewise-affine kernel and finite node-value changes.',degenerate='N:The theorem assumes a<b, so constant-length-zero integration is outside this statement.',endpoints='C:Proof3.2 isolates finite quadrature nodes, including endpoints, and invokes finite-point invariance for the kernel-product integral.',choice='N:Proof1.1 uses finite-order Taylor induction and Proof3.1 interchanges a supplied finite quadrature with integrals; no arbitrary node selection occurs.')
note('thm-na-euler-maclaurin-composite-trapezoid-remainder','Euler-Maclaurin derives an explicit finite remainder formula and upper bound, not an equivalence of smoothness and error order.',empty='N:N>=1 panels and p>=1 corrections are explicit, so neither principal finite sum has an empty input range.',zero='N:The panel count N and correction order p are positive; no step divides by a zero panel count.',one='C:At p=1 the first integration-by-parts boundary term and J_2 already give the stated formula; N=1 leaves one panel.',degenerate='N:a<b and N>=1 imply h>0, as required by the panel coordinate u_i.',endpoints='C:Proof1.2 uses equality of Bernoulli endpoint values to cancel interior interfaces, retaining derivative differences at a and b.',choice='N:The panel grid and Bernoulli recursion are fixed; the remainder is constructed by finite integration-by-parts identities.')
note('cor-na-composite-trapezoid-even-power-expansion','The corollary gives a level-independent remainder bound for the first j trapezoid powers; it asserts no converse from observed errors.',empty='N:j>=1 and N>=1 supply nonempty correction and panel ranges in the stated expansion.',zero='N:Neither j=0 nor N=0 is part of this corollary, even though the separate Romberg algorithm permits level0.',one='C:Putting j=1 in Proof1.1 invokes Euler-Maclaurin with p=2 and retains its h^4 remainder.',degenerate='N:The inherited a<b hypothesis keeps each panel length positive.',endpoints='C:The coefficient c_(j+1) uses the two stated endpoint derivatives; Proof2.1 bounds that coefficient without changing endpoints.',choice='N:Proof2.1 explicitly defines epsilon_N=c_(j+1)h^(2j+2)+rho_N and C_j; no minimizing coefficient is chosen.')
note('lem-na-closed-newton-cotes-eight-panel-weights-and-exactness','The cardinal-weight lemma establishes exactness through degree9; it does not characterize every rule with degree9 exactness.',empty='N:Each P_j and D_j contains the eight other indices of the fixed nine-node set.',zero='C:The zero polynomial is included among degree-at-most8 polynomials and the linear quadrature/integral both vanish.',one='C:Proof3.1 includes the constant polynomial1 and its cardinal interpolant, proving sum_j w_j=2.',degenerate='N:The node spacing1/4 makes every factor in D_j nonzero.',endpoints='C:Proof2.1 integrates the endpoint cardinals at j=0,8 and matches their symmetric weights.',choice='N:Proof1.1 explicitly multiplies the fixed cardinal factors; uniqueness in Proof3.1 identifies the interpolant without selecting nodes.')
note('thm-na-romberg-even-power-cancellation','The cancellation theorem assumes a smooth even-power expansion and proves a remainder order; observed error ratios are explicitly excluded as a converse.',empty='C:Proof4.1 reaches j=m, where the remaining coefficient sum from m+1 to m is empty.',zero='N:The theorem requires m>=1, whereas its base column j=0 is only the start of the proof induction.',one='C:At m=1, Proof2.1 cancels c_1 using the factor (4-4)/(4-1)=0.',degenerate='N:The smooth-expansion theorem assumes a<b; zero interval length is not an admitted Romberg error-order instance.',endpoints='C:Column0 inherits the closed trapezoid samples, and the expansion coefficients retain endpoint derivative differences.',choice='N:Proof3.1 gives an explicit recurrence for C_j from C_(j-1); no error coefficient is selected by measured ratios.')
note('cor-na-closed-newton-cotes-eight-panel-peano-bound','The corollary applies the Peano norm estimate to the verified degree9 rule; it makes no converse claim about C^10 smoothness.',empty='N:The Peano kernel has the fixed nine quadrature terms j=0,...,8.',zero='C:For zero f the tenth derivative and quadrature residual are both zero in Proof2.1.',one='C:For f=1 the tenth derivative vanishes and degree9 exactness makes the residual zero.',degenerate='N:The domain is fixed[-1,1], with positive length and fixed nonzero factorial denominators.',endpoints='C:Proof1.1 evaluates the truncated-power antiderivative at t and1 for every t in[-1,1].',choice='N:The kernel is explicitly integrated in Proof1.1 and then substituted into a fixed supplier theorem.')
note('thm-na-clenshaw-curtis-interpolation-coefficients','DCT coefficients reconstruct the unique Lobatto interpolant; uniqueness of that polynomial is not a biconditional criterion.',empty='N:n>=1 provides at least two samples; the outer Chebyshev reconstruction always has n+1 terms.',zero='N:The theorem explicitly excludes n=0 before dividing by n; midpoint quadrature is defined in another item.',one='C:For n=1 both coefficients are endpoint coefficients X_k/(2n), and no interior coefficient case is used.',degenerate='N:Proof3.1 uses strict decrease of cosine on[0,pi] to ensure n+1 distinct nodes.',endpoints='C:Proof2.1 treats the half weights at j=0,n separately and obtains exactly y_j at both endpoints.',choice='N:The coefficients are explicit transforms of supplied samples; uniqueness only identifies their polynomial with the canonical interpolant.')
note('def-na-clenshaw-curtis-quadrature','Clenshaw-Curtis quadrature is defined by moments plus a separate midpoint convention, not by an equivalence.',empty='C:At n=0 the rule is assigned (b-a)f(m) rather than applying DCT formulas to an empty interior grid.',zero='C:The explicit zero-degree branch is the midpoint rule and uses no division by n.',one='C:At n=1, moments mu_0=2,mu_1=0 and the two DCT samples give the endpoint trapezoid.',degenerate='N:a<b is required, so the affine scale d is positive.',endpoints='C:The affine Lobatto pullback sends t=1 to b and t=-1 to a; n=0 instead samples the midpoint.',choice='N:Samples, DCT coefficients and moment sums are explicit once the supplied function and degree are fixed.')
note('thm-na-clenshaw-curtis-exactness-and-near-best-error','The theorem proves polynomial exactness and a near-best upper bound; it does not infer smoothness or polynomiality from a small error.',empty='C:Proof1.1 handles n=0 using the constant midpoint interpolant, without requiring interior Lobatto nodes.',zero='C:For n=0, Proof1.1 integrates the constant f(m) and Proof2.1 reproduces degree0 polynomials.',one='C:The n=1 branch uses the two-node Lobatto interpolant; the same integral-of-interpolant argument remains valid.',degenerate='N:The affine pullback is used only for a<b, so d>0 and its inverse is defined.',endpoints='C:Proof1.1 integrates on both full closed intervals and Proof3.1 uses their affine bijection, including both endpoints.',choice='N:Proof3.1 compares two infima by an explicit polynomial pullback bijection; it need not choose a best approximant.')
note('alg-na-computed-clenshaw-curtis-via-fast-dft','The computed rule defines a finite transform call with status propagation; it asserts neither accuracy equivalence nor converse stability.',empty='C:At n=0 the first branch returns the midpoint sample and never constructs the even extension.',zero='C:The n=0 branch computes (b-a)y_0 and bypasses all DFT and division-by-n steps.',one='C:At n=1 the extension has length2 with samples y_0,y_1; no reflected interior samples are appended.',degenerate='N:The input requires a<b and valid finite FFT controls; failure to meet transform conditions is a returned status.',endpoints='C:For n>=1 the extension contains each endpoint sample once before reflecting only indices1 through n-1.',choice='N:Every transform control is supplied; an unavailable DFT returns its status rather than selecting a hidden exact transform.')
note('ex-na-square-root-endpoint-breaks-romberg-order','The example proves a positive h^(3/2) lower bound for one Richardson column, not an iff characterization of all nonsmooth inputs.',empty='C:Proof2.1 explicitly treats the sum of later panels as empty when N=1.',zero='N:N>=1 gives h>0; the zero endpoint value sqrt0 is explicitly used rather than a zero panel count.',one='C:At N=1, Proof1.1 supplies the only-panel excess and Proof2.1 has no later-panel terms.',degenerate='N:The square-root example is fixed on[0,1], not a collapsing interval.',endpoints='C:Proof3.1 isolates the singular derivative at0, while the quadrature itself uses the defined endpoint value sqrt0=0.',choice='N:The defect uses explicit panel inequalities and a finite tail comparison, with no fitted extrapolation coefficient.')
note('ex-na-eight-panel-newton-cotes-negative-weight-amplification','The example gives a sharp perturbation norm and a separate smooth Peano bound; it does not state an iff test.',empty='N:The perturbation vector has exactly nine entries indexed0 through8.',zero='C:When epsilon=0 every perturbation is zero, and Proof1.1 gives zero quadrature perturbation.',one='C:The fixed nine-node coefficient sum is2, verified in Proof1.1; the example has no variable one-node rule.',degenerate='N:The interval and nine-node stencil are fixed, so no variable mesh collapse is considered.',endpoints='C:The first and last sample perturbations carry the endpoint weights989/14175 in the absolute-weight sum.',choice='C:Proof1.1 constructs the sharp witness explicitly by delta_j=epsilon sign(w_j), with either value allowed only for a zero weight.')
note('ex-na-clenshaw-curtis-integrates-quadratics','The example computes three polynomial integrals for n=2; it is not a converse statement about arbitrary data.',empty='N:Its sample vector has exactly three entries at1,0,-1.',zero='C:The odd polynomial x has sample vector(1,0,-1) and integral zero in Proof2.1.',one='C:Proof2.1 computes the constant function1 using samples(1,1,1), obtaining2.',degenerate='N:The fixed reference interval[-1,1] and three distinct nodes do not collapse.',endpoints='C:Proof1.1 gives endpoint weights1/3 at1 and-1 and middle weight4/3 at0.',choice='N:The three sample vectors and cosine values are explicitly substituted; there is no choice of quadrature nodes.')
