note('lem-fractional-integral-kernel-power-integral','The kernel lemma evaluates a convergent integral for beta>0,t>0; it makes no iff assertion about other exponents.',empty='N:t>0 fixes a nonempty integration interval; this is not a node-family assertion.',zero='N:The stated beta>0 and t>0 exclude beta=0 and t=0; an integral at zero time is defined separately in fractional-integral items.',one='C:At beta=1 the kernel is1 and the formula is the ordinary integral t.',degenerate='N:A collapsed time interval is excluded by t>0.',endpoints='C:Proof1.1 substitutes on compact truncations and passes to endpoint limits for0<beta<1; this is not an unjustified ordinary integral through the singular endpoint.',choice='N:Proof1.1 uses the explicit substitution x=(t-s)/t and Proof2.1 evaluates the fixed Beta-Gamma factors.')
note('def-caputo-derivative-and-fractional-integral','The item defines fractional integration and Caputo differentiation on the specified L1/AC classes; it does not identify all continuous functions with Caputo solutions.',zero='C:The continuous-input estimate has value0 at t=0; the order restrictions beta>0 and0<alpha<1 remain in force.',one='C:Order beta=1 gives the ordinary indefinite integral because Gamma1=1; alpha=1 is excluded from the Caputo branch.',degenerate='N:T>0 is explicit; no zero-length time interval is an input.',choice='C:The Definition expressly separates the Countable-Choice AC derivative theorem from inputs with separately supplied integrable derivative; it does not silently infer L1 differentiability without that assumption.')
note('lem-continuous-fractional-integral-is-holder','The fractional-integral lemma proves a sufficient Hölder estimate and continuity, not a converse characterization of Hölder functions.',empty='N:T>0 and a continuous g on[0,T] supply a nonempty domain including0.',one='N:The order hypothesis is0<beta<1; beta=1 is outside this Hölder lemma, even though ordinary integration is defined elsewhere.',degenerate='N:The time interval is positive and beta is strictly between0 and1.',endpoints='C:Proof1.1 gives I^beta g(0)=0 and Proof1.2 treats all0<=s<=t<=T before symmetry.',choice='N:The difference estimate compares two explicitly given kernel integrals and takes their uniform bound; no maximizing time is selected.')
note('lem-fractional-integral-semigroup','The semigroup lemma establishes composition of positive-order integral operators and their L1 bound; it states no converse implication.')
note('alg-uniform-and-graded-grid-l1','L1 defines a finite recurrence on an admitted increasing grid, not an equivalence or global graded-rate theorem.',choice='N:The grid is supplied, or is explicitly power-graded with supplied exponent r; each scalar update is determined by previous values and returns solve-failed if uncertified.')
note('alg-backward-euler-convolution-quadrature','Backward-Euler CQ defines weights and a finite scalar recurrence, without an iff accuracy or memory-truncation assertion.',endpoints='C:The procedure explicitly initializes V^0=0,U^0=u_0 and returns all nodes through the supplied N; forcing is queried only at positive indices.',choice='N:Weights and updates are determined in increasing index order from supplied data, with invalid-input and solve-failed statuses rather than hidden inverse choices.')
note('lem-caputo-volterra-equivalence-for-absolutely-continuous-solutions','This item is a genuine two-way AC solution equivalence, whose implication rows remain with the contradiction owner.',empty='N:T>0 makes(0,T) and[0,T] nonempty and a specified AC function u is supplied.',one='N:The derivative order is strictly0<alpha<1; alpha=1 is not claimed by this equivalence.',degenerate='N:The hypotheses exclude T=0,alpha=0,alpha=1; no fractional kernel at those orders is asserted.',endpoints='C:Proof1.1 upgrades almost-everywhere equality of continuous representatives to all[0,T], using a one-sided positive-length interval if the disputed point is an endpoint.')
note('lem-l1-positive-weights-and-discrete-comparison','The L1 lemma proves weight positivity, comparison, residual stability and unique updates, not their converse hypotheses.',empty='N:The strictly increasing grid starts0 and endsT>0, hence has at least one positive-width cell.',one='C:At n=1 the middle coefficient sum in Proof2.1 and4.1 is empty; the diagonal b_1,1+lambda is still positive.',degenerate='N:Every h_k>0 follows from strict grid increase, and0<alpha<1 gives an integrable last-cell kernel.',endpoints='C:Proof1.2 explicitly checks the singular product on the first cell n=k=1 using the integrable Beta kernel; q^0=0 is retained.',choice='N:Proof2.1 takes the first negative index in a finite sequence, and Proof4.1 determines each update by a positive scalar coefficient.')
note('lem-weakly-singular-fractional-gronwall','Fractional Gronwall gives a series majorant from the integral inequality; it is not an iff characterization of that inequality.')
note('lem-backward-euler-cq-weights-and-partial-sums','CQ weight signs, generating function and positive partial-sum bounds are consequences of the fixed recurrence, not an equivalence assertion.',one='C:Proof1.1 gives omega_1=-alpha; hence p_1=1-alpha, matching the finite product and Gamma ratio.',degenerate='N:The strict range0<alpha<1 excludes the zero and unit-order endpoints of the binomial-weight family.',endpoints='C:The generating identity is asserted only for |z|<1, while the index endpoint n=0 has p_0=1 by its explicit empty-product convention.',choice='N:The recurrence fixes every omega_j and the Gamma equation fixes p_n; the proof constructs no arbitrary coefficient family.')
