note('lem-parabolic-weak-compactness-separable-bochner-hilbert','The weak-compactness lemma proves subsequential compactness and a closed graph Hilbert realization; it is not an iff characterization of separability.',empty='C:Proof1.1 expressly retains the original sequence when the orthonormal family is empty, which is the zero-space branch.',zero='C:For X={0} every sequence is the zero sequence and converges weakly to0; the empty orthonormal expansion is consistent with Proof1.1.',one='C:For a one-dimensional X the coordinate subsequence selection in Proof1.1 is finite, and Proof3.1 identifies its scalar weak limit.',degenerate='C:A finite-dimensional dense-span construction may exhaust after finitely many vectors; Proof1.1 distinguishes finite and infinite orthonormal families.',endpoints='N:The primary compactness assertion is about Hilbert vectors and weak derivative graphs, not a pointwise time-endpoint estimate.',choice='C:Proof1.1 expressly uses Countable Choice for nested coordinate subsequences and the diagonal; Proof1.2 retains it for representatives and a summable subsequence.')
note('lem-parabolic-zero-boundary-sobolev-space-is-separable','The Sobolev lemma constructs the separable evolution triple and equivalent gradient norm; it is not an iff criterion for arbitrary open sets.',endpoints='N:The claim concerns spatial function spaces and embeddings; it supplies no time endpoint value or time discretization.')
note('thm-parabolic-finite-dimensional-measurable-ode','The measurable Galerkin ODE theorem gives a unique energy-bounded solution from finite-dimensional coercive data; the hypotheses are not asserted necessary.',empty='C:Proof1.1 treats V_h={0} before choosing a basis, giving the sole path u_h=0.',zero='C:Zero initial state and zero load give u_h=0 by uniqueness and the energy bound in Proof3.1.',one='C:For dim(V_h)=1, Proof1.1 produces one scalar measurable coefficient ODE and Proof2.1 solves it on the same finite contraction partition.',degenerate='C:The zero-dimensional case is handled in Proof1.1; a zero matrix B also has a separate any-partition clause in Proof2.1.',endpoints='C:Proof2.1 concatenates the finite interval solutions from the supplied initial value, and Proof3.1 integrates the energy bound from0 to every t.',choice='N:Proof1.1 fixes one finite orthonormal basis and Proof2.1 uses finitely many contraction intervals; it does not choose witnesses from a countable collection of nonempty sets.')
note('lem-parabolic-energy-pairing-and-trace','The energy-pairing lemma derives representative, trace and integration-by-parts identities; it does not state a converse membership criterion.',empty='N:The energy-space input is a supplied pair of paths on positive interval[0,T]; even zero spaces contain those zero paths.',zero='C:For u=v=0 the energy, product and trace right-hand sides all vanish.',one='C:In the scalar evolution triple the identities reduce to the usual derivative of u squared and the scalar product rule.',degenerate='C:If s=t, both endpoint differences and both integrals are zero, as allowed by0<=s<=t<=T.',endpoints='C:Proof2.2 passes uniform H convergence to both arbitrary endpoints s,t, including0 andT; Proof4.1 fixes the representative uniquely.',choice='C:Countable Choice is retained from the graph-norm approximation supplier; Proof2.1 additionally selects one time with norm below its mean-square average.')
note('thm-parabolic-backward-euler-error-and-reconstruction','The averaged backward-Euler theorem proves stability, reconstruction and first-order nodal bounds under its regularity; it is not an iff rate theorem.',zero='C:When initial value and load are zero the discrete nodes are zero by the unique-step result; e^0=0 and u_h prime=0 also make C_e=0.',one='C:For N=1 the sums in Proof2.1 and4.1 have one increment, and the reconstruction integral in Proof3.1 still gives its factor tau_1/3.',degenerate='C:If V_h={0}, its variational steps have the sole zero solution; positive coercivity and positive cell widths remain admissible.',endpoints='C:Proof2.1 distinguishes the maximum over every endpoint m from the cumulative bound at m=N; Proof3.1 uses the full initial jump.',choice='N:Proof1.1 determines every step by a square matrix with zero kernel and explicitly says no choice axiom is added; later bounds sum those fixed nodes.')
note('thm-parabolic-crank-nicolson-second-order','The CN theorem gives sufficient H2-in-time nodal order and stability estimates; it expressly does not infer that regularity from the method definition.',zero='C:With zero data the unique CN sequence is zero; when the exact trajectory is affine in time the second derivative term in C_e vanishes.',one='C:At N=1 Proof2.2 uses the single-cell Green kernel, whose bound tau_1/4 yields the same stated residual estimate.',degenerate='C:A zero-dimensional V_h has the unique zero step; for nonzero V_h coercivity excludes a singular step matrix in Proof1.1.',endpoints='C:The Green kernel in Proof2.2 vanishes at both cell endpoints, and the separate nodal maximum includes m=0 and m=N.',choice='N:Proof1.1 explicitly solves a fixed finite-dimensional linear system without a choice axiom; the residual kernel is then written explicitly.')
note('lem-parabolic-ritz-projection-well-defined','The Ritz lemma proves unique solvability, linearity and boundedness of its specified projection, not an iff projection characterization.',empty='C:Proof1.1 assigns R_h u=0 when V_h={0}, before a nonempty finite basis is required.',zero='C:With u=0 the unique Ritz solution is0; Proof2.1 handles a zero projected vector before dividing by its norm.',one='C:A one-dimensional V_h gives one scalar stiffness coefficient a(e_1,e_1)>0 in Proof1.1.',degenerate='C:The zero trial-space branch is explicit; a nonzero homogeneous solution is excluded by positive coercivity, not assumed absent.',endpoints='N:This is a fixed spatial linear-map statement without a time or mesh-endpoint limit.',choice='N:Proof1.1 fixes one finite ordered basis and an invertible matrix; the Statement expressly adds no choice axiom.')
note('thm-parabolic-variational-wellposedness-and-energy-stability','The variational theorem derives an energy solution from separable measurable coercive forms, not a necessary-and-sufficient condition on all parabolic problems.',empty='C:Proof1.1 treats V={0}; density then forces H={0}, leaving the sole zero data and solution.',zero='C:Zero f and u_0 give the zero solution; Proof7.1 invokes uniqueness for the corresponding zero functional.',one='C:The Hilbert-space argument permits V=H=R; its space-time BNB proof and scalar form retain the same positive coercivity constants.',degenerate='C:Proof1.1 removes the zero-space case before the BNB construction; vanishing w is separately exempted from normalization in Proof2.1.',endpoints='C:Proof6.1 uses bounded trace convergence to enforce u(0)=u_0 and derives the energy inequality for every tau in[0,T].',choice='C:Countable Choice is explicit: Proof4.1 chooses initial approximants, Proof5.1 and6.1 unite countable dense-test null sets, and the weak compactness supplier retains it.')
note('thm-parabolic-fem-semidiscrete-existence-energy','The FEM theorem instantiates the finite-dimensional variational ODE and energy bound; it is not a converse mesh-regularity characterization.',zero='C:Zero initial trial vector and zero restricted load produce the zero path by the finite-dimensional supplier used in Proof2.1.',one='N:Spatial dimension1 is outside this mesh theorem, whose Statement explicitly assumes d>=2; a one-dimensional trial subspace is still allowed.',degenerate='N:The matching affine simplicial mesh uses nondegenerate cells; a collapsed simplex is not an admitted finite-element mesh.',endpoints='C:Proof2.1 imports the supplied initial trace u_h(0)=u_h^0 and the all-t energy estimate from the finite-dimensional theorem.',choice='C:Proof1.1 explicitly retains Countable Choice with the separable Sobolev evolution triple; the finite load restriction in Proof2.1 adds no extra choice principle.')
note('thm-parabolic-semidiscrete-ritz-error-splitting-and-energy-bound','The error-splitting theorem derives a full residual identity and energy bound, with Ritz cancellation only on its stated branch; it is not an iff optimality criterion.',empty='C:A zero finite trial space makes P_h=0 and u_h=0, so theta=0; its dual residual is the zero functional and the tested equation is vacuous.',zero='C:When u=u_h=0 the explicitly defined rho,theta and residual all vanish; the positive form constants are unchanged.',one='N:The inherited finite-element geometry has d>=2, so a one-dimensional physical domain is not part of this theorem; a single trial basis function is allowed.',degenerate='N:The inherited matching affine mesh excludes collapsed simplices; merely measurable time dependence does not erase the full residual.',endpoints='C:Proof3.1 retains theta(0) in the integrated energy estimate, and Proof1.1 uses the supplied continuous representative for P_h u.',choice='C:Countable Choice is retained in the continuous solution and product-rule suppliers; Proof1.1 uses only finite-basis measurability for the residual itself.')
note('cor-parabolic-semidiscrete-spatial-error-rates','The rate corollary combines primal approximation, two target-specific adjoint estimates and residual energy control; it does not assert unconditional full adjoint regularity.',zero='C:Ritz initialization gives theta(0)=0 in Proof3.1; if the target and its derivative are zero all displayed approximation errors vanish.',one='C:The endpoint s=1 is explicitly the full-adjoint branch, giving h^(r+1); r=1 remains an allowed polynomial approximation order.',degenerate='N:The inherited shape-regular affine mesh excludes collapsed cells; the common positive dual/coercivity constants remain hypotheses.',endpoints='C:Proof3.1 fixes the initial Ritz error to zero; the full-adjoint parameter endpoint s=1 is included while s=0 is excluded.',choice='C:Countable Choice is retained from the real scalar finite-element and evolution hypotheses; Proof1.1 takes an infimum estimate and does not choose a best approximant.')
note('ex-parabolic-crank-nicolson-time-regularity-order-loss','The scalar CN example shows one H1 trajectory violates the claimed H2-dependent rate; it is a counterexample, not an iff regularity classification.',empty='N:The example fixes a scalar triple, interval[0,1], and a positive first time step.',degenerate='N:Its form has fixed M=alpha=1; no zero-coercivity or collapsed-time instance is part of the example.',endpoints='C:Verification1.1 isolates the non-square-integrable second derivative at0, and Verification1.2 starts with U^0=u(0)=0.',choice='N:Verification1.2 solves one explicit scalar step and Verification2.1 takes the prescribed sequence tau=1/N.')
note('ex-parabolic-heat-initial-projection-error','The fan-mesh example calculates two different projections and a nonzero initial splitting error; it is not an iff test of projection equality.',empty='N:The specified mesh has four triangles and one nonzero center-hat basis vector, as verified in1.1.',endpoints='C:Verification3.1 identifies the initial difference P_hu_0-R_hu_0=-phi/15, rather than silently assuming zero initial error.',choice='N:Verification2.1 obtains the two scalar coefficients3/5 and2/3 from explicit integrals; the mesh and basis are fixed.')
note('ex-parabolic-semidiscrete-ritz-error-identity','The Ritz example verifies one error identity on a scalar trial space by direct integration, not an iff characterization of arbitrary projections.',empty='N:Verification1.1 proves the trial space is span{phi} on a fixed four-triangle mesh; it is not empty or zero-dimensional.',endpoints='C:Verification3.1 uses the actual initial Ritz condition to obtain theta(0)=0.',choice='N:Verification1.2 explicitly computes the three integrals, and Verification2.1 fixes the Ritz coefficient2q/3 without a witness-family choice.')
